			<!-- Chapter 9: The Two Bottlenecks (Page 4 of 6) -->
			<section class="book-page" id="page-52">
				<div class="page-body-content">
					<div class="page-running-head">
						<span class="chapter-header-label">Chapter 9: The Two Bottlenecks</span>
						<span class="page-count-label">Page 52</span>
					</div>

					<div class="chapter-content">
						<p>As established in Chapters 4 and 5, receded root margins, cement washouts, and micro-cracks leave millions of dentinal tubules exposed directly to the oral cavity. Each tubule is an open conduit leading straight to the unmyelinated C-fibers of the dental pulp&nbsp;and&nbsp;periodontium.</p>
						<p>When a patient eats carbohydrates - whether a slice of whole wheat bread, a bowl of oatmeal, or a cooked sweet potato - the mouth does not wait for&nbsp;systemic&nbsp;digestion.</p>
						<p>The moment chewing begins, salivary amylase hydrolyzes complex starches into maltose and free glucose directly inside&nbsp;the&nbsp;mouth.</p>
						<p>Microscopic residues of this glucose pack into the gum sulcus, interdental spaces, and open cervical margins. Resident oral bacteria, particularly Streptococcus mutans and Lactobacilli, metabolize that sugar and pump out concentrated&nbsp;lactic&nbsp;acid.</p>
						<p>Instead of lactic acid accumulating internally due to starved blood vessels, the oral bacteria deliver a continuous, external lactic acid wash straight down the open&nbsp;tubule&nbsp;funnels.</p>
						<p>The chemical endpoint is indistinguishable: lactic acid binds to ASIC receptors,&nbsp;trips&nbsp;the</p>

