					<div class="chapter-content">
						<h3>The definitive long-term solution requires active&nbsp;dental&nbsp;restoration:</h3>
						<p>Replacing Failing Crowns: If an older crown (particularly one placed 10 to 15+ years ago) shows signs of distal or mesial cement washout, explorer catch, or dark shadowing, it must be removed. As documented in my lower arch (#36D), what appears as a minor margin flaw on a 2D bitewing can conceal three active cavities decaying quietly beneath the metal casing. Removing the crown, sterilizing the tooth prep, and placing a tightly bonded modern zirconia or porcelain crown permanently eliminates the hidden&nbsp;acid&nbsp;trap.</p>
						<p>Class V Cervical Bonding: Where deep non-carious cervical lesions, notches, or extensive gumline recessions expose bare root dentin, a skilled restorative dentist can place micro-hybrid or flowable composite resin patches. Physically bonding resin over the receded neck covers the 20,000 to 45,000 open tubules per square millimeter, completely cutting off the physical pathway between oral fluid and&nbsp;living&nbsp;pulp.</p>
						<p>When your dental appointments focus on inspecting margins, testing seals, and repairing carpentry rather than reporting vague, unexplained nerve symptoms, your care team becomes a partner in building permanent armor over your&nbsp;nervous&nbsp;system.</p>

					</div>
